"Empty search results\nNo results were found for the provided queries--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2602.12318] Abstractive Red-Teaming of Language Model Character (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12318)\nciteturn32133view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.12318\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 12 Feb 2026]\nL9: # Title:Abstractive Red-Teaming of Language Model Character\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Nate Rahn , cite6†Allison Qi , cite7†Avery Griffin , cite8†Jonathan Michala , cite9†Henry Sleight , cite10†Erik Jones L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Abstractive Red-Teaming of Language Model Character, by Nate Rahn and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite11†View PDF cite12†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We want language model assistants to conform to a character specification, which asserts how the model should act across diverse user interactions. While models typically follow these character specifications, they can occasionally violate them in large-scale deployments. In this work, we aim to identify types of queries that are likely to produce such character violations at deployment, using much less than deployment-level compute.\nL17: To do this, we introduce abstractive red-teaming, where we search for natural-language query categories, e.g. \"The query is in Chinese. The query asks about family roles,\" that routinely elicit violations. These categories abstract over the many possible variants of a query which could appear in the wild.\nL18: We introduce two algorithms for efficient category search against a character-trait-specific reward model: one based on reinforcement learning on a category generator LLM, and another which leverages a strong LLM to iteratively synthesize categories from high-scoring queries.\nL19: Across a 12-principle character specification and 7 target models, we find that our algorithms consistently outperform baselines, and generate qualitatively interesting categories; for example, queries which ask Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct to predict the future lead to responses saying that AI will dominate humanity, and queries that ask GPT-4.1-Mini for essential prison survival items lead to enthusiastic recommendation of illegal weapons.\nL20: Overall, we believe our results represent an important step towards realistic pre-deployment auditing of language model character.\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite13†arXiv:2602.12318 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite14†arXiv:2602.12318v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.12318†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Nate Rahn [cite16†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Thu, 12 Feb 2026 18:12:12 UTC (11,079 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Abstractive Red-Teaming of Language Model Character, by Nate Rahn and 5 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite11†View PDF L37:   * cite12†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite17†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite18†view license†creativecommons.org L41: \nL42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.LG\nL45: \nL46: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L47: \nL48: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2026-02 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite24†cs L53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L